From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:35:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: keep the variants of a dynamic vertex-input
 pipeline on disk

A dynamic vertex-input pipeline compiles a native variant for each
vertex-input state at the first draw that uses it, and a guest that has
run before compiles the same variants again: an Android boot translates
a few hundred of them, and a guest context that is created again, an
app started a second time, starts with an empty table. The MSL of a
variant is a function of what it is built from, not of the process that
built it, so the variants are now kept in Mesa's disk cache.

A variant is looked up by a key over the stripped NIR it is built from
(with the hash of the guest's SPIR-V zeroed, which is the only thing in
it that differs between runs of the same guest), the device and driver
identity, the pipeline's lowered state, the hashes of its layouts and
the vertex-input state. A hit builds the Metal pipelines from the stored
MSL; a miss, as before, translates and then stores. What the compile
reads has to be in the key: an immutable sampler's flags in the layout
hash, and whether the depth attachment is an input attachment in the
state hash, were not, and are now. The content hash of a deferred shader
read back from a pipeline cache blob is never taken from the blob (the
binary format is 3 and carries none), and neither is the primitive a
geometry or tessellation evaluation shader outputs: both are taken again
from the NIR the shader holds, when its parent first asks for them.

An entry is a small descriptor with the shaders' info and one name per
MSL text, and the texts are separate entries named by the hash of their
content, because most variants of a pipeline differ in one shader's text
and share the rest. Nothing in an entry is trusted: the descriptor is
checked for its magic, format, driver build, key, stage count, payload
size and payload hash before it is read, a text for its header, both
lengths, the name it echoes and the hash of its bytes, and a length or
count is checked against what is there before anything is allocated
from it. What a shader's description holds is checked against what a
writer writes before it is used for anything: stage bits, raster
classes, the geometry records (one of the five modes a draw has a Metal
type for, and an index count the advertised geometry limits allow, which
are the output vertices of all the invocations and the restarts of the
primitives), the sample count, and every field that reaches a Metal
descriptor, which aborts the process on a value it does not take. A
variant must also be one of its parent: its frames are the parent's
stages, once each and read to their end, the primitive it outputs is the
one its parent's own shaders output (a geometry or tessellation
evaluation stage decides it, never the record being checked), and the
raster classes it owns are those the parent's state reaches. One load
holds a bounded number of stored text bytes. An entry that fails any
check is removed and counts as a miss, so the variant is translated and
stored again, and a text that is stored but damaged where the cache does
not look is replaced by the next writer instead of kept. The same
description check serves the shaders of a guest's pipeline cache.

disk_cache_put_nocopy frees the data it was given when it cannot make
its job, as it documents.

MESA_KK_VARIANT_CACHE=0 turns the cache off and MESA_KK_VARIANT_LOG=1
logs each lookup, compile, load and store on stderr.

test_variant_entry covers the entry format and its readers without
Metal, test_variant_disk_cache the driver: it runs boots against one
cache directory, damages the entries between them in every way a file
can be damaged, forges descriptors that are whole as bytes and wrong as
shaders, and has two threads record the same first draw. It also
stores, finds and refuses the variants of a geometry parent of 32
invocations and of a tessellation parent, and offers the driver's shader
deserializer the vertex shader record a pipeline cache holds, whole and
edited. test_descriptor_layout_hash covers the layout hash.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
